  "answer_text": "If a recreational drug is legalized, individuals who are currently serving prison sentences for offenses related to that drug **do not automatically get released.** Whether they have to finish serving their time depends on the specific country, the legal jurisdiction, and how the legalization law was written. \n\n---\n\n### 1. The Default Rule: No Automatic Retroactivity\nIn common-law countries like the United States, Canada, and the United Kingdom, the default rule is that **an act is judged by the law as it existed at the time the act was committed**. \n\nEven if an act becomes legal tomorrow, a person who did it yesterday still broke the law when they did it. Under this principle, their conviction and sentence remain legally valid, and they are expected to serve their remaining time.\n\nConstitutions (such as the U.S. Constitution) usually have *ex post facto* clauses. These clauses protect you from being prosecuted if the government makes an act illegal *after* you did it. However, **they do not work in reverse.** There is no constitutional rule stating that you must be freed if the government legalizes an act *after* you were convicted.\n\n### 2. The International Principle of *Lex Mitior*\nIn contrast to the common-law default, some international treaties and civil-law countries recognize the legal principle of **_lex mitior_** (Latin for \"more lenient law\"). Under Article 15 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR), if a law changes to reduce a penalty or decriminalize an act, the offender is supposed to benefit from the more lenient law.\n\nHowever, *lex mitior* has two major limitations:\n* **Finality of Judgments:** In many countries, the principle only applies to active cases\u2014meaning those currently going to trial or on appeal. If a person has already been convicted and their appeals have run out (a \"final judgment\"), they often cannot use *lex mitior* to get out of prison.\n* **National Reservations:** The United States is one of the few nations that attached a formal reservation to the ICCPR, specifically exempting itself from being federally bound by the *lex mitior* principle.\n\n### 3. How Prisoners *Do* Get Released\nEven though release is not automatic, governments frequently choose to release prisoners or clear their records as a matter of policy, calling it **restorative justice** or **retroactive legality**. This is done through several mechanisms:\n\n#### A. Specific Retroactive Provisions in the Legalization Bill\nWhen lawmakers write a legalization bill, they can explicitly include instructions on what to do with past offenders. \n* **Resentencing:** The law can allow current inmates to petition a judge to have their sentences reduced or vacated entirely. \n* **Expungement:** The law can dictate that past criminal records for the newly legalized drug be erased or sealed. \n* *Example:* When states like California, Illinois, and New York legalized recreational cannabis, they built provisions into the laws to automatically or systematically expunge hundreds of thousands of low-level marijuana convictions and resentence or release those still incarcerated.\n\n#### B. Executive Clemency (Pardons and Commutations)\nIf the legalization bill does not address past convictions, the head of the executive branch (such as a President, Prime Minister, or State Governor) can use their personal power of clemency. \n* **Commutation:** Shorter or end a prison sentence immediately.\n* **Pardon:** Forgive the crime and restore a person's civil rights.\n* *Example:* In the United States, President Joe Biden issued a blanket pardon for federal offenses of simple marijuana possession, though this primarily cleared records rather than releasing prisoners, as very few people are in federal prison for *simple* possession alone.\n\n### 4. Why Some People Remain in Prison\nEven when a drug is legalized and retroactive relief is offered, many people convicted of charges relating to that drug stay in prison. This happens for several reasons:\n\n* **Distinction of Charges (Possession vs. Trafficking):** Legalization bills almost always draw a line. While individuals convicted of simple possession are often released or pardoned, those convicted of large-scale trafficking, manufacturing, or selling to minors are rarely let out.\n* **Sentence Enhancements:** If a drug charge was paired with other offenses\u2014such as carrying a firearm, committing an act of violence, or money laundering\u2014the inmate will usually have to serve out the remainder of their sentence for those associated crimes.\n* **Administrative Hurdle (The \"Petition\" System):** In many jurisdictions, release is not automatic. Inmates must formally petition a court, find a lawyer, and go through a hearing. Prisoners who lack legal resources or whose paperwork gets bogged down in court backlogs can remain locked up for months or years after the drug is officially legalized.",
